    ('therm_combustion_fuels', 'Calorimetry of alcohols', 'thermochem,combustion,process', 'C2H5OH + O2 + water-bath', 'process', 'f:blue;h:hot;n:burn 0.5 g under 100 mL of water in a copper calorimeter and the temperature rise gives about -1370 kJ/mol against the true -1367 - with the same 5 % error from radiation that every generation of students has; the homologous series trend (per CH2 about 660 kJ) is the takeaway;c:none', 'app=copper-calorimeter,spirit-lamp;tag=cbse11-calorimetry'),
    ('therm_solubility_curve', 'KNO3 solubility at four temperatures', 'thermochem,equilibrium,practical,process', 'KNO3 + H2O', 'process', "p:white-crystal;n:crystallisation reappears at a fixed temperature for a fixed concentration, so four points give the solubility curve, and the curve's steepness is why potassium nitrate is recrystallised and sodium chloride is evaporated;c:none", 'T=20-80;app=thermometer,water-bath;tag=cbse11-solubility'),
    ('therm_crystallisation_water', 'Water of crystallisation by weighing', 'thermochem,gravimetric,process', 'Na2CO3.xH2O + heat', 'process', 'p:none;h:hot;n:heat to constant mass in a crucible; a 2.86 g sample losing 2.06 g gives x = 10 exactly - the arithmetic of the hydrate formula with a real balance and a real error bar;c:none', 'T=300;app=crucible,pipeclay-triangle,balance;tag=cbse-empirical-formula'),
    ('therm_enthalpy_of_solution_trend', 'Dissolution enthalpies down group 2', 'thermochem,dissolution,process', 'MgSO4 + CaSO4 + BaSO4 + H2O', 'process', 'n:solubility falls down the group while lattice enthalpy also falls - it is the HYDRATION enthalpy that falls faster, which is the actual explanation the sim can compute from the thermo tables instead of asserting;c:none', 'tag=cbse11-group2'),
    ('therm_cold_pack_design', 'Design a cold pack that reaches 0 C', 'thermochem,stoich,practical,process', 'NH4NO3 or urea + H2O', 'process', 'h:ice-cold;n:100 mL of water at 20 C needs about 12 g of ammonium nitrate (or 22 g of urea, which is less cold but safer to keep at home) to reach 0 C - a design problem with a number the engine computes from dH_soln and Cp;c:none', 'tag=application'),
    ('therm_neutralisation_weak', 'Why the weak-acid end point is basic', 'thermochem,acid_base,titration', 'CH3COOH + NaOH', 'CH3COONa + H2O', 'h:warm;n:the pH at the equivalence point is 8.7 not 7, so the indicator must change there; the app should plot the whole curve from Ka, not jump to the end point, because the buffer region at pH = pKa is the exam question;c:none', 'app=pH-probe;tag=cbse11-titration'),
    ('equ_haber_lechatelier', 'Ammonia synthesis under pressure', 'equilibrium,gas,pinned,pinned', 'N2 + 3 H2', '2 NH3', 'n:compressing at constant T increases the ammonia fraction (4 moles of gas become 2) but cooling increases it more - and the compromise at 450 C exists only because the catalyst is useless cold;c:none', 'T=450;P=200;dH=-92.2;tag=cbse11-equilibrium'),
    ('equ_no2_syringe', 'NO2 in a syringe: compression and colour', 'equilibrium,gas,pinned,pinned', '2 NO2', 'N2O4', 'c:red-brown->paler-after-peak;n:push the plunger in and the brown first darkens (concentration up) then lightens (pressure shifts it to dimer) - the two-step response that separates Le Chatelier understanding from memorisation;c:none', 'app=syringe;tag=cbse11-equilibrium'),
    ('equ_fepo4_ksp', 'Which precipitates first: hydroxide or phosphate?', 'equilibrium,precip,process', 'Fe(3+) + OH(1-) + PO4(3-)', 'process', 'n:with Ksp(FePO4)=1e-22 versus Fe(OH)3 4e-38 the hydroxide wins at neutral pH, which is why iron is unavailable to plants in alkaline soil while phosphate lock-up is why P fertiliser is banded not broadcast;c:none', 'tag=application-soil'),
    ('equ_fracsol_ag2cro4', 'Why chromate works as a Mohr indicator (the arithmetic)', 'equilibrium,precip,titration,process', 'Ag(1+) + Cl(1-) + CrO4(2-)', 'process', 'p:white-then-brick-red;n:the free Ag+ needed to start Ag2CrO4 is 3.3e-6 M while AgCl finishes at 1.3e-5 M - the end point sits a hair after equivalence, and computing that is the whole lesson;c:none', 'tag=cbse12-ionic'),
    ('equ_salting_out', 'Salting out soap and proteins', 'equilibrium,practical,extraction,process', 'soap-solution + NaCl', 'process', "p:floats-crumbly;n:adding solid NaCl raises [Na+] so far that the sodium-carboxylate solubility product is exceeded and the soap separates and floats - the same common-ion trick used to precipitate casein and to 'salt out' DNA at home;c:none", 'tag=cbse12-cleansing'),
    ('equ_distribution_law', 'Distribution of iodine between water and CCl4', 'equilibrium,extraction,process', 'I2 + H2O + CCl4', 'process', 'c:brown->violet-organic;n:the ratio [I2]org/[I2]aq stays about 85 whatever the starting concentration - so a single 20 mL extraction beats three 7 mL ones, and the app can prove it numerically;c:none', 'app=stoppered-tube;tag=cbse12-extraction'),
    ('equ_solubility_temp', 'Potassium chlorate vs sodium chloride with cooling', 'equilibrium,solubility,practical,process', 'KClO3 + NaCl + H2O + heat + cool', 'process', 'p:crystal-vs-nothing;n:make saturated solutions hot and stand them in ice: KClO3 drops from 20 to 4 g/100 g and crystallises heavily while NaCl barely moves (37.5 to 35.7) - the separation of a mixture by exactly this difference;c:none', 'T=100-0;tag=cbse11-solubility'),
    ('equ_common_ion_hcl', 'Passing HCl gas into saturated NaCl', 'equilibrium,gas,precip,process', 'NaCl(sat) + HCl(g)', 'process', "p:white-crystal;n:'the solubility of salt is REDUCED by hydrogen chloride' is the ionic-product argument, and the same effect is why concentrated HCl crystals out of itself;c:none", 'danger=2;tag=cbse11-ionic'),
    ('equ_hemoglobin_altitude', 'Oxyhaemoglobin at altitude', 'equilibrium,biological,process', 'Hb + O2', 'process', 'n:the lower pO2 at 3000 m shifts the equilibrium left, so saturation falls; acclimatisation raises 2,3-BPG and the haemoglobin count - the app can plot the saturation curve against pO2 for sea level and altitude, which is Le Chatelier with a pulse;c:none', 'tag=application-physiology'),
    ('equ_edta_pH_dependence', 'Why EDTA titration needs a pH 10 buffer', 'equilibrium,complex,titration,process', 'H2Y(2-) + Ca(2+) + OH(1-)', 'process', 'n:at pH below 7 most EDTA is protonated and its apparent constant collapses, so the end point drifts - the buffer is not a formality but the reason the titration works, and the engine can show K_apparent against pH;c:none', 'app=pH-probe;tag=cbse12-complexometric'),
    ('equ_precip_selective', 'Selective precipitation: 0.01 M Cu2+ and 0.01 M Zn2+ together', 'equilibrium,precip,process', 'Cu(2+) + Zn(2+) + H2S + H(1+)', 'process', 'p:black-only;n:at 0.3 M H+ the [S2-] is about 1e-22 M, so Q exceeds Ksp for CuS (6e-37) but not for ZnS (2e-25) - copper comes down alone and the zinc waits for group IV. That is the entire logic of the H2S scheme expressed as two numbers;c:none', 'tag=analysis-group2'),
    ('org_lucas', 'Lucas test: the turbidity clock for alcohols', 'organic_test,kinetics,process', 'C4H10O + HCl + ZnCl2', 'process', 'c:clear->cloudy;t:seconds-to-none;n:tertiary goes cloudy at once, secondary in about 5 minutes, primary shows nothing at room temperature - the alkyl chloride is insoluble, so the test is a rate measurement disguised as a colourless-to-cloudy change;c:none', 'T=25;tag=cbse12-alcohol'),
    ('org_sodium_alcohol', 'Sodium on ethanol vs water (the acidity comparison)', 'organic_test,redox,process', 'C2H5OH + Na', 'process', 'g:h2;l:fizz;n:effervescence is gentler than with water and the residue (sodium ethoxide) makes the solution strongly alkaline with phenolphthalein - the direct demonstration that an alcohol is a weaker acid than water;c:none', 'danger=2;tag=cbse12-alcohol'),
    ('org_schiff', "Schiff's reagent for aldehydes", 'organic_test,process', 'RCHO + fuchsin-SO2', 'process', "c:colourless->magenta;n:the aldehyde restores the quinonoid colour while a ketone does nothing - and the colour survives adding sulphuric acid only for aldehydes (the 'pararosaniline' confirm); formalin gives a blue-white product, not the magenta of acetaldehyde;c:none", 'tag=cbse12-aldehyde'),
    ('org_tollens_vs_fehling_ketone', 'Propanone: negative on both aldehyde reagents', 'organic_test,process', 'CH3COCH3 + Tollens + Fehling', 'process', "c:no-change;n:both stay blue/silver-free; the sim should report 'no reaction' with the same prominence as a positive - half the skill of qualitative analysis is knowing what nothing looks like;c:none", 'T=60;tag=cbse12-carbonyl'),
    ('org_molisch', 'Molisch test for all carbohydrates', 'organic_test,carbohydrate,process', 'carbohydrate + alpha-naphthol + H2SO4', 'process', 'p:purple-ring;c:none;n:conc acid dehydrates any sugar to furfural/HMF which couples with the naphthol - so a purple ring appears for glucose, starch AND cellulose: a group test, not an identity test, and the app should say so;c:none', 'danger=2;tag=cbse12-biomolecules'),
    ('org_seliwanoff', 'Seliwanoff: aldose vs ketose', 'organic_test,carbohydrate,process', 'C6H12O6 + resorcinol + HCl + heat', 'process', 'c:colourless->cherry-red;n:fructose gives the red within 30 seconds because ketoses dehydrate faster; glucose needs minutes and gives a faint pink - the timing, not the colour, is the diagnostic and that is why the stopwatch matters;c:none', 'T=100;time=1minute;tag=cbse12-biomolecules'),
    ('org_barfoed', "Barfoed's test: monosaccharide vs disaccharide", 'organic_test,carbohydrate,process', 'glucose + sucrose + Cu-acetate', 'process', "p:red-brick-vs-none;n:copper acetate in acetic acid is acidic enough that only a monosaccharide reduces it within 10 minutes; a disaccharide needs heating far longer - 'same colour, different clock' and a rare example where the observation IS the rate;c:none", 'T=100;time=10minutes;tag=advanced'),
    ('org_brown_paper_chrom', 'Chromatographic Rf of sugars with aniline dip', 'separation,organic,analysis,process', 'sugar-mixture + butanol-pyridine-water + aniline', 'process', "p:brown-spots;n:three solvents and three reagents, four spots; Rf = distance of spot / distance of front is dimensionless so the app can grade the student's own strip; aniline phthalate needs 100 C to develop;c:none", 'T=100;time=2hours;tag=cbse12-chromatography'),
    ('org_xanthoproteic', 'Xanthoproteic test for aromatic rings in protein', 'organic_test,protein,process', 'protein + HNO3 + NH4OH', 'process', "c:white->yellow->orange;n:nitration of the tyrosine/tryptophan rings gives the yellow which turns orange with alkali - and the same reaction stains skin yellow, which is why nitric acid on the fingers is a permanent 'I did the test' mark;c:none", 'danger=2;tag=cbse12-biomolecules'),
    ('org_biuret', 'Biuret test for peptide bonds', 'organic_test,protein,process', 'protein + NaOH + CuSO4', 'process', 'c:blue->violet;p:none;n:it is the Cu2+ coordinating four peptide nitrogens in alkali, so free amino acids give no colour while a hydrolysed protein fades - the test counts bonds, not residues, which the app can state exactly;c:none', 'tag=cbse12-biomolecules'),
    ('org_millon', "Millon's test for tyrosine", 'organic_test,protein,process', 'protein + Hg/Hg2(NO3)2 in HNO3 + heat', 'process', 'c:->brick-red;n:mercury-nitrate nitrates and complexes the phenol ring; gelatin gives nothing because it has no tyrosine - the one protein test with a built-in exception, which is worth a record of its own;c:none', 'T=80;danger=3;safety=display-only;tag=cbse12-biomolecules'),
    ('org_lead_acetate_protein', 'Sulphur in protein (the sodium-fusion follow-up)', 'organic_test,analysis,process', 'protein + Na + S2- + Na2Fe(CN)5NO', 'process', 'c:->violet-pink;n:sodium sulphide from the fusion gives the violet nitroprusside colour, and lead acetate gives the black PbS - the two confirmations for sulphur-containing amino acids (cysteine, methionine);c:none', 'T=60;tag=analysis'),
    ('org_grease_spot', 'Lipids: the translucent spot and the acrolein smell', 'organic_test,lipid,process', 'oil + paper + KHSO4 + heat', 'process', 's:pungent-acrid;translucent-spot;l:none;n:the grease spot does not disappear on drying (unlike water), and heating oil with potassium hydrogen sulphate gives acrolein, the acrid aldehyde that makes burnt fat smell - a lipid test with both a physical and a chemical half;c:none', 'T=180;tag=cbse12-biomolecules'),
    ('org_iodine_value', 'Unsaturation of oils: iodine number', 'organic_test,redox,extraction,process', 'oil + I2-KI + Na2S2O3', 'process', "c:brown->colourless;n:olive oil takes up far less iodine than linseed - the difference in 'iodine number' is exactly the difference between a drying and a non-drying oil, which is why one varnishes and the other fries;c:none", 'T=25;time=30minutes;tag=cbse12-lipid'),
    ('org_saponification_hardsurface', 'Making and testing your own soap', 'organic_synth,practical,process', 'oil + NaOH + ethanol + NaCl', 'process', "p:curds;n:reflux with ethanolic NaOH (the ethanol keeps oil and brine in one phase), salt out the soap, wash it, then test three things: lather in soft water, scum in hard water, pH with litmus - a complete 'product you can use' project;c:none", 'T=90;time=30minutes;tag=cbse12-cleansing'),
    ('org_carbylamine', 'Carbylamine test for primary amines', 'organic_test,process', 'C6H5NH2 + CHCl3 + KOH', 'process', 's:foul;n:isocyanide (carbylamine) is made and it smells appallingly - the test is kept because no other common reaction gives that smell, but it is a demonstration in a hood, never a student practical, and the sim says so;c:none', 'T=60;danger=3;safety=display-only;tag=analysis'),
    ('org_azo_coupling', 'Azo dye from benzenediazonium and phenol', 'organic_synth,organic_test,process', 'C6H5N2(1+) + C6H5OH + NaOH', 'process', 'p:orange-red;n:coupling happens at the para position in alkaline solution (phenoxide is the activated partner); the colour is a conjugated -N=N- chromophore, so the sim can highlight why extending conjugation shifts the colour to red;c:none', 'T=0;tag=cbse12-dye'),
    ('org_acetylation', 'Acetylating salicylic acid to aspirin', 'organic_synth,condensation,process', 'C7H6O3 + (CH3CO)2O', 'process', 'p:white-crystal;c:none;n:two drops of phosphoric acid, 70 C for ten minutes, then water to destroy the excess anhydride - and the FeCl3 test on the crystals tells you whether unreacted phenol survived, which is the yield-and-purity lesson in one experiment;c:none', 'T=70;time=25minutes;yield=70;app=water-bath;tag=cbse12-preparation'),
    ('org_hydroboration_ref', 'Anti-Markovnikov hydration (reference)', 'organic_synth,process', 'propene + borane then H2O2/OH-', 'process', 'n:the sim shows the product distribution difference against acid hydration without pretending to run a glovebox; a synthetic target the user can compare, not a bottle;c:none', 'danger=2;safety=reference-only'),
    ('org_kmn_glycol', 'Cold permanganate on ethene (the glycol route)', 'organic_synth,redox,process', 'C2H4 + KMnO4 + H2O', 'process', "c:purple->brown;p:brown;n:ethylene glycol in the flask and MnO2 on the filter - the 'Baeyer test' and a synthesis are literally the same reaction, which is a far better point than the test alone;c:none", 'tag=cbse12-alkene'),
    ('org_alkane_halogenation', 'Methane with chlorine in sunlight', 'organic_synth,substitution,process', 'CH4 + Cl2 + light', 'process', 'c:green-fades;s:pungent;p:oily-droplets;n:the green chlorine colour goes and oily chloromethane/methylene chloride droplets condense with HCl fuming at the mouth - a free-radical chain that stops dead in the dark, which is the whole mechanism evidence in one tube;c:none', 'danger=2;light=UV;tag=cbse12-haloalkane'),
    ('org_cannizzaro', 'Cannizzaro disproportionation (no alpha-H aldehyde)', 'organic_synth,redox,process', 'HCHO + NaOH', 'process', 'c:->colourless;n:formaldehyde with concentrated alkali gives methanol and sodium formate because it has no alpha hydrogen to enolise - the reaction students get wrong by applying aldoxide chemistry everywhere, and the app should let them try that;c:none', 'T=25;danger=2;tag=cbse12-aldehyde'),
    ('org_aldol', 'Aldol condensation of ethanal', 'organic_synth,condensation,process', 'CH3CHO + dilute-NaOH', 'process', "p:yellow-oil;n:dilute alkali joins two molecules to 3-hydroxybutanal and warming loses water to crotonaldehyde - 'an aldehyde that is also a nucleophile' is the hardest idea in the chapter, and the reaction only exists because of the alpha hydrogen;c:none", 'T=25;tag=cbse12-carbonyl'),
    ('org_haloform_oxid', 'Haloform oxidation of ethanal', 'organic_test,redox,process', 'CH3CHO + 3 I2 + 4 NaOH', 'process', "p:yellow;s:antiseptic;n:the aldehyde is oxidised to formate while the methyl group leaves as CHI3; the same reagent (I2 + NaOH, i.e. 'sodium hypoiodite') is why a positive iodoform says nothing about whether you started with an aldehyde or a secondary alcohol;c:none", 'T=60;time=5minutes;tag=cbse12'),
    ('org_hofamide', 'Hofmann degradation: amide to amine with one carbon less', 'organic_synth,redox,process', 'CH3CONH2 + Br2 + NaOH', 'process', "g:?,s:ethylamine;c:none -> pale;n:methylamine escapes as a gas with a fishy smell and its hydrochloride crystallises from the alkaline solution - a degradation that actually explains 'how do you shorten a chain', which most syllabi mention without any way to see it;c:none", 'danger=2;tag=advanced'),
    ('org_dehydration_acid_vs_alumina', 'Two routes from ethanol, two products', 'organic_synth,dehydration', 'C2H5OH', 'C2H4 + H2O', 'g:ethene;c:none;n:350 C over alumina is elimination; the same alcohol with concentrated H2SO4 at 413 K gives the ether instead - the app can put the two temperature regimes side by side, which is the exam question in picture form;c:none', 'T=350;tag=cbse12-alcohol'),
    ('org_oxidation_1deg', 'Primary alcohol to acid with acidified dichromate', 'organic_synth,redox,process', 'C2H5OH + K2Cr2O7 + H2SO4', 'process', 'c:orange->green;reflux;n:reflux drives it to ethanoic acid while distilling as soon as it forms stops at the aldehyde - the separation is a boiling-point argument (21 vs 118 C), not a reagent argument, and the app can show both;c:none', 'T=80;danger=2;tag=cbse12-alcohol'),
    ('ind_contact', 'Sulphuric acid by the contact process (full chain)', 'industrial,redox,equilibrium,pinned,pinned', '4 FeS2 + 11 O2', '2 Fe2O3 + 8 SO2', "g:so2;n:then 2 SO2 + O2 -> 2 SO3 over V2O5 at 450 C / 1.5 atm and 99.5 % H2SO4 absorbs the SO3; the sim should run the three stages in sequence with the yields, because 'the conditions are a compromise' is the point;c:none", 'T=450;cat=V2O5;tag=cbse11-industry'),
    ('ind_ostwald', 'Nitric acid from ammonia (Ostwald)', 'industrial,redox,pinned,pinned', '4 NH3 + 5 O2', '4 NO + 6 H2O', 'c:none;g:no;n:Pt-Rh gauze at 800 C, 96 % yield in a flash; then 2 NO + O2 -> 2 NO2 and absorption in water with excess air; the app can show why the process needs a cool second stage (equilibrium) while the first needs heat (rate and selectivity);c:none', 'T=800;cat=Pt-Rh;tag=cbse11-industry'),
    ('ind_haber', 'Ammonia plant with recycle', 'industrial,equilibrium,pinned,pinned', 'N2 + 3 H2', '2 NH3', 'n:only about 15 % converts per pass, so the unreacted gases are recycled - and the reason the whole plant is a loop rather than a tube is an equilibrium limitation the student can compute;c:none', 'T=450;P=200;dH=-92.2;cat=Fe-K2O;tag=cbse11-industry'),
    ('ind_mercury_cell', 'Mercury vs diaphragm vs membrane cells', 'industrial,electrolysis,pinned,pinned', '2 NaCl + 2 H2O', '2 NaOH + Cl2 + H2', 'g:cl2;g:h2;n:all three make the same products; the difference is purity and pollution - mercury cells gave 50 % NaOH but Minamata, diaphragm cells give carbonate-contaminated caustic, membranes are the modern answer;c:none', 'E0=2.19;tag=application-industry'),
    ('ind_downs', 'Sodium metal by the Downs cell', 'industrial,electrolysis,pinned,pinned', '2 NaCl', '2 Na + Cl2', 'n:molten NaCl with CaCl2 lowers the melt to about 600 C (pure NaCl melts at 801) and the cell separates the products with a steel hood - the app can show why aqueous NaCl cannot give sodium metal (E = -2.71 vs water) as a computation;c:none', 'T=600;tag=cbse12-electrochem'),
    ('ind_hall_heroult', 'Aluminium by molten-salt electrolysis', 'industrial,electrolysis,pinned,pinned', '2 Al2O3 + 3 C', '4 Al + 3 CO2', 'n:dissolved in cryolite at 950 C so the cell runs at 4-5 V and 150 kA; the carbon anodes are consumed (which is why CO2 not O2 forms) and about 400 g of alumina per coulomb-scale makes the industry an electricity-price business;c:none', 'T=950;E0=4.5;tag=cbse12-metallurgy'),
    ('ind_blast_furnace', 'Iron in the blast furnace', 'industrial,redox,pinned,pinned', 'Fe2O3 + 3 CO', '2 Fe + 3 CO2', 'h:hot;p:slag;n:CO is the actual reducer, the tuyere reaction C + CO2 -> 2 CO regenerates it, and limestone removes silica as CaSiO3 slag - the sim can run the four zones with their temperatures (900/1200/1500/2000 C) and let the user see where each equation applies;c:none', 'T=1500;tag=cbse11-metallurgy'),
    ('ind_solvay', 'Solvay cycle: the recycled ammonia', 'industrial,equilibrium,process', 'CaCO3 + NaCl + 2 NH3 + CO2 + H2O', 'process', 'p:white-crystal;n:the clever part is that the ammonia is regenerated by CaO + 2 NH4Cl -> CaCl2 + 2 NH3 + H2O and only the CaCl2 brine is waste - a process designed around an equilibrium and a recycle loop, which is what the sim should show;c:none', 'T=15;tag=cbse11-industry'),
    ('ind_water_gas_shift', 'The shift reaction and the catalyst', 'industrial,equilibrium,redox', 'CO + H2O', 'CO2 + H2', 'n:Fe3O4/Cr2O3 at 350 C for the high-temperature shift (80 % conversion) then Cu/ZnO at 210 C to finish it for ammonia feed - the app can show that the same equilibrium is driven by removing CO2 with water under pressure, i.e. two levers at once;c:none', 'T=350;dH=-41;cat=Fe3O4;tag=application-industry'),
    ('ind_ethylene_cracking', 'Steam cracking of ethane', 'industrial,decomposition,organic', 'C2H6', 'C2H4 + H2', 'n:850 C, 10 % steam, 0.1 s contact - and the steam is there to keep the coke off the tubes, so the equilibrium favours products at low pressure and high temperature while the furnace is limited by coking;c:none', 'T=850;dH=137;tag=cbse12-industry'),
    ('ind_polymerisation', 'Addition polymerisation of ethene', 'industrial,organic,polymer,process', 'n C2H4', 'process', 'p:waxy-solid;n:LDPE needs 1500 atm and peroxide initiation (radical, branched, 110 C) while HDPE uses Ziegler-Natta at 6 atm and 70 C (linear, higher density) - same monomer, same repeat unit, different material; the app should show the density and the branch difference;c:none', 'P=1500;T=110;tag=cbse12-polymers'),
    ('ind_nylon', 'Nylon-6,6 at the interface', 'industrial,polymer,condensation,process', 'hexamethylenediamine + adipoyl chloride', 'process', "p:film-at-interface;n:the two solutions are immiscible so the polymer forms only at the seam and can be pulled up continuously on a wire - 'the nylon rope trick', and a condensation reaction that runs at room temperature because the acid chloride does not need heat;c:none", 'app=beaker,wire;tag=cbse12-polymers'),
    ('ind_soap_industry', 'Continuous saponification with brine', 'industrial,process', 'fat + NaOH + NaCl', 'process', 'p:curds-on-top;n:the plant does the same three steps a student does - boil with caustic, salt out, then finish with perfume and filler - and the glycerol is recovered from the spent lye, which is where a fair part of the profit lives;c:none', 'T=100;time=1hour;tag=application-industry'),
    ('ind_biodiesel', 'Transesterification of oil to biodiesel', 'industrial,organic,extraction,process', 'oil + CH3OH + NaOH', 'process', 'p:two-layers;n:base catalysis at 60 C gives the fatty-acid methyl esters on top and glycerol below; if the oil has free fatty acids the soap forms instead and the yield collapses - which is why used cooking oil needs titration first, and that is a real calculation;c:none', 'T=60;time=1hour;tag=application-renewable'),
    ('ind_esterification_yield', 'Ester yield and the reflux trap', 'industrial,organic,equilibrium', 'CH3COOH + C2H5OH', 'CH3COOC2H5 + H2O', 'n:Kc is only about 4, so an equimolar mix reaches 67 % yield and removing water (or using excess alcohol) is what makes it a process - the app can compute the yield against the excess so the student can find the optimum;c:none', 'T=78;Kc=4;time=1hour;app=reflux,sep-funnel;tag=cbse11-equilibrium'),
    ('env_hh_alkalinity', 'Alkalinity of tap water (two end points)', 'titration,environment,process', 'HCO3(1-) + H(1+) + phenolphthalein + methyl-orange', 'process', 'c:pink-fades,then-yellow;n:phenolphthalein alkalinity counts carbonate, the methyl-orange end point counts carbonate + bicarbonate; the two numbers together tell you whether the water is carbonate- or hydroxide-alkaline - a real water-works calculation from two indicators;c:none', 'app=burette;tag=application-environment'),
    ('env_chlorine_demand', 'Chlorine demand of a water sample', 'environment,redox,titration,process', 'Cl2 + NH2Cl + KI + thiosulphate', 'process', "c:colourless->brown->end;n:dose increasing chlorine into identical samples, let stand 30 minutes, then iodometrically measure the residual; the first break where free chlorine appears is the 'break-point' and the shortfall is the demand - the experiment that decides how much bleach to add after a flood;c:none", 'time=30minutes;app=iodometric;tag=application-environment'),
    ('env_ph_acid_rain', 'Measuring rain and lake water pH', 'environment,acid_base,process', 'rain + H2O + CO2 + SO2 + NO2', 'process', "n:unpolluted rain is about pH 5.6 (carbonic acid), acid rain 4.2-4.8 (nitric and sulphuric); the app should refuse to report 'pH 7 = pure water' as neutral-in-nature, because that misconception is exactly what the reading disproves;c:none", 'app=pH-probe;tag=application-environment'),
    ('env_do_winkler', 'Dissolved oxygen: the Winkler steps', 'environment,redox,titration,process', 'Mn(2+) + OH(1-) + O2', 'process', 'c:white->brown;p:brown;n:Mn(OH)2 (white) is oxidised by the dissolved O2 to brown MnO(OH)2, which liberates iodine from KI in acid, and the thiosulphate titration of that iodine equals the oxygen; the whole chain is one equation per step and the app can run it as a chain;c:none', 'app=bottle,burette,starch;tag=application-environment'),
    ('env_bod_incubation', 'BOD5 at 20 C in the dark', 'environment,kinetics,biological,process', 'sample + seed + O2 + 5days', 'process', "n:measure DO on day 0 and day 5 in the dark at 20 C, and the difference diluted is the BOD - a five-day kinetics experiment whose 'rate law' is microbial growth, which is why the standard fixes T, time and darkness;c:none", 'T=20;time=5days;tag=application-environment'),
    ('env_phosphate_detergent', 'Phosphate in a detergent by the molybdate blue method', 'environment,analysis,extraction,process', 'PO4(3-) + MoO4(2-) + ascorbate', 'process', "c:colourless->molybdenum-blue;n:after filtering out the turbidity, ascorbate reduces the molybdophosphate to the blue heteropoly complex and the colour is read at 880 nm; that is the exact method that made 'phosphate-free' measurable - and it is why the ban worked;c:none", 'T=25;time=20minutes;tag=application-environment'),
    ('env_hardness_boiler', 'Lime-soda softening of a bulk water', 'environment,precip,practical,process', 'Ca(2+) + Mg(2+) + Ca(OH)2 + Na2CO3', 'process', "p:white-flakes;n:lime takes the magnesium down as Mg(OH)2 and soda takes the calcium as CaCO3; both must be dosed to the milliequivalents of each present, and the 'excess lime' left behind makes the water hard again - an overdosing failure the app can let you commit and observe;c:none", 'app=jar-test;time=30minutes;tag=application-water'),
    ('env_orm_water', 'Iron and manganese in groundwater (aeration + greensand)', 'environment,redox,extraction,process', 'Fe(2+) + O2 + MnO2-catalyst', 'process', "c:clear->yellow->rust;n:'clear at the tap, rust-stained after an hour in the bath' is oxidation and precipitation in the pipes; the greensand catalyst does the manganese which air alone cannot, and the app can show the two different rate limits;c:none", 'time=1hour;tag=application-water'),
    ('env_soil_ph', 'Soil pH and why it is measured in KCl not water', 'environment,acid_base,extraction,process', 'soil + H2O / soil + 1M-KCl', 'process', 'n:the KCl displaces exchangeable Al3+ and H+ so the reading is 0.5-1 pH unit lower than the water paste - which is the whole reason lime recommendations use the salt extraction; a measurement-method lesson rather than a reaction, but a genuinely useful one;c:none', 'app=pH-probe;tag=application-soil'),
    ('env_lime_test_water', 'Which water sample clouds limewater fastest?', 'environment,gas_test,comparison,process', 'CO2 + Ca(OH)2', 'process', "p:milk-white;n:exhaled breath, a fermenting sugar flask, and vinegar+bicarbonate all cloud it at different rates - the app can rank them and it turns 'the gas we exhale is CO2' into a measurement instead of a statement;c:none", 'app=boiling-tubes;time=2minutes;tag=icse10-respiration'),
    ('tr_group1_water', 'Li, Na, K with water as a comparison series', 'trend,redox,process', 'Li + H2O / Na + H2O / K + H2O', 'process', "f:crimson/yellow/lilac;h:hot;n:the rate and the violence increase down the group while the flame colour of each metal shows up as well - the app should run all three in one panel so the trend is a picture, and note that lithium's is the only one that does not melt;c:none", 'danger=3;tag=cbse11-group1'),
    ('tr_group2_water', 'Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba with water and with steam', 'trend,redox,process', 'Mg + Ca + Ba + H2O', 'process', "g:h2;l:bubbles;n:cold water: Mg nothing, Ca steady, Sr fast, Ba violent; steam: Mg burns. The hydroxides' solubility increases down the group in the same direction the reactivity does - two trends in one panel, and the reason 'Mg(OH)2 is milk of magnesia while Ba(OH)2 is a strong alkali' is consistent;c:none", 'danger=2;tag=cbse11-group2'),
    ('tr_halogen_displace', 'The halogen displacement ladder in one strip', 'trend,redox,analysis,process', 'Cl2-water + Br2-water + I2-water + KCl/KBr/KI + hexane', 'process', 'c:layer-colours;n:six wells: Cl2/Br- and Cl2/I- positive, Br2/I- positive, all the reverse pairs negative - a complete oxidising-power series with negative controls in a 3x3 grid the engine can generate automatically;c:none', 'app=spotting-tile;tag=cbse11-halogens'),
    ('tr_oxidation_states', 'Chlorine oxyanions: the disproportionation ladder', 'trend,redox,equilibrium,process', 'Cl2 + OH(1-) cold/hot', 'process', 'n:cold dilute alkali gives Cl- + ClO- (bleach); hot concentrated gives Cl- + ClO3- - the same element, the same reagent, different temperature, and it is the clearest possible illustration that which product you get is a question of conditions;c:none', 'T=75;tag=cbse11-p-block'),
    ('tr_mn_oxides', 'Manganese oxidation states as a set', 'trend,redox,coordination,process', 'Mn(2+) + MnO4(1-) + MnO4(2-) + MnO2', 'process', 'n:colourless(+2), black-brown dioxide, green manganate(+6) and purple permanganate(+7), all interconvertible by pH and by adding a reducer - the app can lay the four bottles in a row and let the user drive each transition;c:none', 'tag=cbse12-d-block'),
    ('tr_fe_co_cu_colors', 'The first-row metals by colour and by hydroxide', 'trend,coordination,analysis,process', 'Ti V Cr Mn Fe Co Ni Cu Zn + NaOH', 'process', 'n:with dilute NaOH: white (Ti, Mn then brown), blue (Cu), green (Ni, Fe then brown), pink (Co), violet (Cr) - the hydroxide-colour chart is the d-block chapter, and the sim should let a user build it as a 3x4 array of wells;c:none', 'app=spotting-tile;tag=cbse12-d-block'),
    ('tr_inert_pair', 'Lead(II) vs lead(IV), tin(II) vs tin(IV)', 'trend,redox,process', 'PbO2 + SnCl2 + HCl', 'process', 'c:->colourless/white;n:the +4 state of lead is the oxidiser (PbO2 -> Pb2+) while for tin the +2 state is the reducer (Sn2+ -> Sn4+) - the inert pair effect as two opposite reactions in one tube, which is how it is actually taught in the lab; note the PbCl2 white precipitate forming too;c:none', 'danger=2;tag=cbse11-p-block'),
    ('tr_lanthanide_contr', 'Cerium(IV) as a titrant because it wants the +3 state', 'trend,redox,titration,process', 'Ce(4+) + Fe(2+)', 'process', 'c:yellow->pale,near-colourless;n:Ce4+ + Fe2+ -> Ce3+ + Fe3+ is a clean one-electron, one-proton-free reaction with E = +1.61 V in acid, so it titrates iron without any indicator other than ferroin - and it works because the +4 state is unstable;c:none', 'app=burette;tag=advanced'),
    ('tr_transition_catalysis', 'Vanadium(V) cycling in the contact process', 'trend,redox,catalysis', 'V2O5 + SO2', 'V2O4 + SO3', 'n:the catalyst is consumed in step one and regenerated in step two (V5+/V4+ couple), so the app can show the two half-reactions adding to the overall 2 SO2 + O2 -> 2 SO3 - that is what a catalyst actually is, expressed as a sum of equations;c:none', 'T=450;tag=cbse12-catalysis')
